    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "I measure the effect of Covid-19 on migration flows and real estate markets within and across US cities. Using near real-time data from Zillow and the US Postal Service, I establish three key results. First, both households and businesses reallocate away from zip codes that are close to a city’s center, have a high share of residents who can work-from-home (WFH), or have high density. Second, this reallocation occurs across many cities when measured with rental rates, but only holds for “superstar” cities when measured through property prices. Third, the within-metro reallocation from city centers to their suburbs is stronger than the between-metro reallocation from dense metros to sparse metros. I rationalize these findings by building a simple spatial equilibrium model where hybrid WFH, as opposed to full-time WFH, generates qualitative predictions that match the empirical patterns. My results suggest that Covid- induced WFH has led to a persistent shift in the spatial allocation of labor.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
